{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ea2703de38ffb937","type":"text","text":"Basic Authentication","x":-920,"y":290,"width":714,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cadccbd95d9ee282","type":"text","text":"- Sends a username and password request, encoded in Base64 (but not encrpyted)\n- Includes a username and password in the HTTP headers of each API request for authentication.\n- not secure and can be easily decoded\n- Always use HTTPS (TLS) for security\n- The username/password are sent in the format username:password, encoded in Base64. Ex: jeremy:ccna would be sent as amVyZW150mNjbme=\n- You can encode/decode Base64 at https://www.base64decode.org/","x":-920,"y":350,"width":714,"height":290},
		{"id":"ce16a7686a57afa6","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260813163527.png","x":-920,"y":640,"width":714,"height":240},
		{"id":"4216f3d9ac990a09","type":"text","text":"The red line in the headers is the auth in Base64 encoding","x":-920,"y":880,"width":714,"height":50},
		{"id":"55e21393cae6b220","type":"text","text":"- Advantage\n\t- Simple and easy to implement\n\n- Disadvantage\n\t- Since credentials are sent in every request, attackers could steal them if the connection is not properly secured\n\t- Even if using HTTPS for encryption, relying solely on a username/password combination isn't particularly secure.","x":-920,"y":1140,"width":714,"height":310},
		{"id":"4345ed67027bd444","type":"text","text":"Types of REST API authentication","x":504,"y":-200,"width":514,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"1fbd2d44c782a8e3","type":"text","text":"- REST APIs use various types of authentication to verify client identity and secure access to resources\n\t- These are called methods or schemes.","x":504,"y":-140,"width":514,"height":99},
		{"id":"e41df6ffdd8bfec0","type":"text","text":"API key authentication","x":935,"y":284,"width":915,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c0c503b6622601d9","type":"text","text":"- API key authentication uses a static key issued by the API provider.\n\t- The client uses this key in each API call for authentication.\n\t- Unlike bearer tokens, the API key is static and remains valid until revoked.\n- API keys can be sent in:\n\t- The HTTP Authorization header (recommended)\n\t- The URL (e.g. add ?api_key=abcd1234 to the end of the URL)\n\t\t- not recommended! URLS are often logged by web servers, proxies, browsers, etc.\n\t- A cookie (sometimes used for browser-based APIs).","x":935,"y":344,"width":915,"height":290},
		{"id":"ee0c44ac6a26bb77","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260813172013.png","x":935,"y":634,"width":913,"height":183},
		{"id":"e6d6848f8d6b7e74","type":"text","text":"OAuth 2.0","x":1995,"y":284,"width":915,"height":55,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"0b017efed3b08804","type":"text","text":"- A secure framework that grants access via access tokens, commonly used for delegated access and third-party authentication.\n\t- provides access delegation, granting third-party applications limited access to resources on behalf of the resource's owner.\n\t\t- There is no need to share the resource owner's credentials with the third party.\n- Examples:\n\t- Logging with google: many websites and apps offer the option to login using your google account\n\t- Connecting apps to social media accounts like instagram, fb, linkedin, etc.\n\t- Calendar integration: a 3rd party tool can be given access to your google calendar to check availability and schedule meetings.","x":1995,"y":339,"width":915,"height":295},
		{"id":"cc67cf69342ab677","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260813181600.png","x":1995,"y":634,"width":915,"height":478},
		{"id":"972f4de716a437b9","type":"text","text":"- Advantages:\n\t- More secure than Basic authentication (no need to transmit the same username/password for every API call)\n\t- Tokens expire, so a stole token will only be temporarily valid\n- Disadvantages\n\t- if a token is stolen, the attacker can access the API until it expires.\n\t  Tokens need to be refreshed periodically, adding extra complexity to implement.\n\t- Should only be used with HTTPS","x":-79,"y":1134,"width":915,"height":310},
		{"id":"c52e720f0305871f","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260813170743.png","x":-79,"y":634,"width":914,"height":296},
		{"id":"aa71e54a6c580b75","type":"text","text":"- Advantages:\n\t- Easier to implement than Bearer authentication (no need to refresh tokens).\n\t- Good for tracking API usage. Often used by cloud services and third-party APIs\n- Disadvantages\n\t- if stolen, the key grants full access until revoked.\n\t- API keys must be rotated manually to maintain security, whereas tokens expire automatically.","x":935,"y":1134,"width":915,"height":310},
		{"id":"f200ac131fee2755","type":"text","text":"Bearer authentication","x":-80,"y":284,"width":915,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"20fd10b6c6b8030e","type":"text","text":"- Uses a token (bearer token) as an HTTP header in each the request to verify the client's identity. (Uses a token instead of a username/password)\n\t- The client first obtains a token by authenticating with an authorization server.\n\t\t- This can be done using using Basic authentication or another method.\n\t- For each API call, the client includes the token in the HTTP Authorization header.\n\t\t- e.g. Authorization: Bearer ya29.a0\\<aRrdaM8\n- The term beater means that anyone who possesses the token can use it.\n\t- If an attacker steals the token, they can make API calls as if they were the legitimate user.\n\t- To mitigate against this, tokens expire after a set period of time.\n","x":-80,"y":344,"width":915,"height":340},
		{"id":"38bc0af1f29eb8a5","type":"text","text":"REST API Authentication","x":636,"y":-400,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"f15fdfc6ece2df00","type":"text","text":"Authentication","x":-280,"y":-709,"width":514,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"859462d871669ed8","type":"text","text":"- Is the process of validating the identity of a user or system to ensure legitimate access to ressources.\n\t- In the case of an API, the resource is the applicatoin and its data.\n- Without authentication, unauthorized users can send API requests, potentially accessing sensitive data or modifying the application.\n\t- Implementing a reliable authentication method is essential for protecting applications and data\n- Many APIs track usage for analytics and billing purposes\n\t- e.g. charging customers according to how much they use the API","x":-280,"y":-649,"width":514,"height":559},
		{"id":"e5d14a5d69d2317f","type":"text","text":"Summary","x":1200,"y":-761,"width":795,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"c2113c28bdc2eb3e","type":"text","text":"- REST API authentication ensures only authorized users or systems can access an API.\n\t- without authentication, unauthorized users could access or modify data.\n- Four types (methods/schemes) of REST API authentication:\n\t- Basic Authentication\n\t\t- Uses a *username and password* encoded in Base64, but not encrypted.\n\t\t- Requires HTTPS (TLS) for security since cleartext credentials are sent with each request.\n\t- Bearer Authentication\n\t\t- Uses a bearer token, granted by an Auth server, instead of a username/password for authentication.\n\t\t- Tokens expire after a sort time but can be stolen if not protected\n\t- API Key Authentication\n\t\t- Uses a static key issued by the API provider for authentication\n\t\t- Good for tracking API usage\n\t\t- Easier to implement but less secure since stolen keys remain valid until revoked\n\t- OAuth 2.0\n\t\t- Provides access delegation, allowing 3rd party apps limited access to resources.\n\t\t- Uses access tokens that expire and can be refreshed (with a refresh token) without user reauthentication.\n\t\t- Four main parties:\n\t\t\t- Resource owner\n\t\t\t- Client App\n\t\t\t- Auth Server\n\t\t\t- Resrource server","x":1200,"y":-701,"width":795,"height":660}
	],
	"edges":[
